\section{Synthetic Calibration at Matched Scale}
\label{app:synthetic_calibration}

We generate synthetic data at matched scale ($N = 8$ models, $I = 176$ items, $J = 9$ judges) with known ground-truth parameters across 20 random seeds. Joint G-MFRM consistently outperforms sequential estimation: $\theta$-RMSE is halved ($0.083$ vs.\ $0.166$), and ranking agreement improves by 7.2 percentage points (mean $\tau = 0.954$ vs.\ $0.882$). Across 20 seeds, joint estimation improves or matches sequential in all cases (10 wins, 0 losses, 10 ties; Wilcoxon signed-rank $p < 0.002$). The synthetic effect size ($\Delta\tau = +0.072$) closely matches the observed real-data effect ($\Delta\tau = +0.071$). All 40 MCMC fits converge ($\hat{R} = 1.0$, ESS $\geq 1{,}678$).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Metric} & \textbf{Sequential} & \textbf{Joint G-MFRM} & \textbf{Improv.} \\
\midrule
$\theta$ RMSE & $0.166 \pm 0.050$ & $0.083 \pm 0.024$ & $-50.1\%$ \\
$\theta$ $\tau$ & $0.882 \pm 0.112$ & $0.954 \pm 0.048$ & $+7.2$pp \\
$c_n$ RMSE & $1.399 \pm 0.358$ & $0.552 \pm 0.328$ & $-60.5\%$ \\
$c_n$ Pearson & $0.850 \pm 0.141$ & $0.866 \pm 0.151$ & $+1.9$\% \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Synthetic calibration at matched scale ($N = 8$, $I = 176$, $J = 9$, 20 seeds). Joint G-MFRM halves $\theta$-RMSE and reduces $c_n$-RMSE by 61\% relative to sequential estimation.}
\label{tab:synthetic_calibration}
\end{table}


\section{CI Coverage Analysis}
\label{app:ci_coverage}

We assess empirical coverage of 95\% posterior credible intervals across synthetic replications. At $N=8$ (49 seeds), ability parameter $\theta$ achieves 90.1\% coverage, approaching the nominal level, while contamination susceptibility $c_n$ achieves only 67.9\%. At $N=50$ (50 seeds; exp-009), $c_n$ coverage improves to 71\%. The coverage gap between $\theta$ and $c_n$ reflects the multiplicative coupling $c_n \cdot e_i$: contamination parameters enter the likelihood through an interaction term, making posterior inference more sensitive to exposure estimation noise and the HalfNormal prior's boundary constraint at $c_n = 0$. These results support treating $c_n$ point estimates and rank orderings as reliable diagnostics, while noting that $c_n$ credible intervals are systematically too narrow.

